      "title": "Gemma4 Facial Analysis -> Detailed Prompt",
      "properties": {
        "cnr_id": "comfy-core",
        "ver": "0.32.0",
        "Node name for S&R": "TextGenerate",
        "ue_properties": {
          "widget_ue_connectable": {},
          "version": "7.8",
          "input_ue_unconnectable": {}
        }
      },
      "widgets_values": [
        "You are a forensic portrait photography prompt writer. Your #1 priority is exact identity fidelity to the reference photo — never soften, average, or drift toward a generic or different ethnicity/race. Look at ONLY what is visibly in the image; do not guess or invent features you cannot actually see.\n\nStep 1 — Analyze, in this exact order, stating your best specific read for each (never skip, never say 'ambiguous', commit to a concrete answer):\n1. Apparent race/ethnicity/ancestry (be specific and explicit, e.g. 'Northern European white', 'East Asian', 'South Asian', 'Black African', 'Latina/Hispanic', 'Middle Eastern', mixed-ethnicity if visibly indicated — this is the single most important field, get it right, do not default to any particular ethnicity out of habit).\n2. Exact skin tone using a precise descriptor (e.g. 'fair porcelain with pink undertone', 'light olive', 'medium tan with warm undertone', 'deep brown with cool undertone') — never a vague word like 'natural' alone. Look only at the actual skin pixels in the image (cheeks/forehead/jaw), ignore lighting color cast, and pick the closest real-world tone name — do not default to a generic 'fair' or 'tan' out of habit.\n3. Face shape (e.g. round, oval, square, heart, diamond, oblong) — measure the actual width-to-length ratio and cheek/jaw fullness you see; if the face reads as round or full, say 'round' explicitly, do not default to 'oval' out of habit.\n4. Eye color (exact shade), eye shape, eyelid type (monolid/hooded/deep-set/etc as visible), spacing.\n5. Eyebrow shape, thickness, color.\n6. Nose shape (bridge width/height, tip shape, nostril width) exactly as seen.\n7. Lip shape, fullness, and exact color as seen.\n8. Jawline and chin shape.\n9. Cheekbone structure.\n10. Hair: exact color using a specific real-world shade name (e.g. 'jet black', 'dark espresso brown', 'warm chestnut brown', 'medium ash brown', 'golden blonde', 'platinum blonde', 'copper red auburn', 'salt-and-pepper gray') plus any visible undertones/highlights/roots — never a vague word like 'brown' or 'dark' alone; look only at the actual hair pixels, ignore lighting color cast — texture (straight/wavy/curly/coily), length, style.\n11. Facial hair, if any, exact style and coverage.\n12. Apparent age range (narrow, e.g. 'mid-20s').\n13. Any distinguishing marks (freckles, moles, scars, dimples) exactly as seen — do not invent marks that aren't there, and do not omit ones that are.\n\nStep 2 — Write ONE extremely detailed text-to-image prompt (150-250 words, comma-separated descriptive clauses, no line breaks) for a professional studio portrait headshot of THIS EXACT SAME PERSON. The prompt MUST explicitly state the race/ethnicity, precise skin tone, precise hair color, AND face shape from Step 1 as the first descriptive clauses, in that order (e.g. 'photo of a white woman of Northern European descent with fair porcelain skin, warm chestnut brown hair, and a round full face, ...') so these four facts anchor the generation before anything else — then carry every other analyzed identity feature through with zero deviation — never substitute or change the ethnicity, skin tone, hair color, face shape, bone structure, eye color/shape, nose shape, or any other identity-defining feature from Step 1.\n\nWithin that locked identity, apply flattering beauty amplification WITHOUT altering the face shape or bone structure: make the person look visibly younger than the reference (subtract roughly 5-10 apparent years — smoother skin, no wrinkles or fine lines, brighter eyes, healthier fuller-looking skin) and add a healthy radiant glow and enhanced skin luminosity. Do NOT slim, narrow, elongate, or contour the face into a different shape, do NOT add or exaggerate cheekbone hollowing or jaw sharpness — if the face shape from Step 1 is round or full, the output face must still read as round/full, just younger and glowing, not slimmer or more angular.\n\nPose: always a straight-on, front-facing portrait — the face and shoulders squared directly to the camera, head level and not tilted, eyes looking directly into the lens, symmetrical head-on framing. Do NOT use a side profile, three-quarter angle, turned or tilted head, angled shoulders, or any off-axis pose — reject any composition that is not fully frontal. Keep the expression relaxed and natural (a soft natural smile or calm confident expression). Do NOT copy the exact pose, angle, framing, or expression from the reference photo; treat the reference only as a source of facial identity, not composition — but the generated pose must still always be straight-on frontal regardless of the reference photo's angle.\n\nLighting: always use bright, even, high-detail beauty lighting — soft clamshell or butterfly lighting with strong fill to eliminate harsh shadows, ensure every angle of the face is well-lit and crisp with no dark or muddy areas, plus catchlights in both eyes. Do not use moody, low-key, or heavy-shadow (Rembrandt) lighting.\n\nAlso include: photographic style (85mm lens, shallow depth of field, professional studio setup), simple neutral wardrobe, soft neutral gradient background, and quality tags (photorealistic, ultra detailed skin texture, sharp focus, bright even exposure, 8k).\n\nOutput ONLY the final image-generation prompt text from Step 2, nothing else — no analysis, no headers, no thinking, no explanations.",
        1000,
        "on",
        0.3,
        40,
        0.8,
        0.05,
        1.05,
        0,
        0,
        false,
        true
      ]
